kosha dissolution

  -- a solute dissolves in a solvent to form a homogeneous solution.
  -- "like dissolves like": polar solvents dissolve polar/ionic solutes.
  --                        non-polar solvents dissolve non-polar solutes.
  --
  -- ionic dissolution: NaCl → Na⁺ + Cl⁻ in water.
  -- water molecules surround each ion (hydration shell) — ion-dipole forces.
  -- lattice energy vs hydration energy determines solubility.
  -- if hydration energy > lattice energy → dissolves. otherwise → insoluble.
  --
  -- solubility: grams of solute per 100g solvent at given temperature.
  -- solubility of solids in water: generally increases with temperature.
  -- solubility of gases in water: decreases with temperature (fizzy drinks go flat).
  --
  -- Henry's law: gas solubility ∝ partial pressure of gas above solution.
  -- Raoult's law: vapour pressure of solution < vapour pressure of pure solvent.
  -- colligative properties: depend on amount of solute, not its identity.
  --   boiling point elevation, freezing point depression, osmotic pressure.
  --
  -- concentration units: molarity (mol/L), molality (mol/kg), mass fraction.
  -- saturation: maximum dissolved solute at equilibrium.
  -- supersaturation: more solute than equilibrium — unstable.

